  urldate = {2026-09-22},
  abstract = {Hierarchical state-space models (HSSMs) offer a promising approach to long-horizon prediction by segmenting sequences into temporal chunks, but their performance hinges on how chunk boundaries are determined. SUNTA argues that chunking should be driven by prediction errors, which more directly indicate when longer-range context becomes necessary. It employs a decoupled training strategy to preserve surprise signals and uses internal inconsistency as a top-down surprise metric to determine chunk boundaries within imagined rollouts. Experiments on 2D and 3D video prediction tasks show that SUNTA uniquely maintains accurate predictions over 250 timesteps, whereas all baselines degrade within the first 10 timesteps.},
